# tests/T27.bd - T27: REMOTE BILLING.
# Does a remote_run() payload burn YOUR budget or the remote box's?
#
# ===========================================================================
# ONE QUESTION
#   When a script launched with remote_run() saturates the CPU it runs on,
#   does the PLAYER'S LOCAL get_cpu() rise (and do local scripts get killed),
#   or is the whole cost charged to the remote box?
#
# WHY IT MATTERS
#   Three separate official statements say "remote", and not one of them has
#   ever been measured:
#     web_api.txt:226  remote_run() "starts the remote script as its own
#                      background process (its own RAM/CPU slot)"
#     web_api.txt:598  server_specs().cpu_ticks is "the rate anything running
#                      ON this box (a daemon dispatch, a remote_run() payload)
#                      actually executes at"
#     web_api.txt:127  a daemon callback runs "on the server's own CPU,
#                      whether you're connected or not"
#   The stake is specific. field_notes.txt:251 retires planted daemons as a
#   variance suspect on the strength of a PLAYER ASSERTION - no `daemons`
#   output was ever pasted anywhere in this investigation. If remote work
#   billed locally, then every remote process, daemon and background job is a
#   source of local load that `ps`/`jobs` can never show, and the central
#   run-to-run mystery (+40.7s / +111.0s / +40.3s / +65.5s / +64.3s) gets a
#   whole new family of invisible causes. If it bills remotely, that family
#   is closed for good and this line of suspicion can be dropped.
#   No script in tests/ has ever called remote_run(), write_file(),
#   server_specs() or daemons(). This is the first.
#
# ===========================================================================
# THE DESIGN - four phases, three step boundaries, ONE identical poll loop
#
#   Every measurement in this file is the SAME 3-line poll:
#       sleep(1) / log("T27 <P> n=" ... cpu=get_cpu()) / counter = counter + 1
#   byte-identical across all four phases and byte-identical (bar the phase
#   letter) to T21.bd's PHASE M, which is itself the loop that read
#   a rock-steady 10.0000001798557 solo in test_cpu_decay.bd. So every number
#   below is directly comparable to the two anchors this programme already
#   has, and the instrumentation is a CONSTANT across the step boundaries -
#   the result is a within-run step change, never a cross-run comparison.
#
#     PHASE Z (4 polls)  local floor, NOT connected           <- anchor
#     ---- connect() ----
#     PHASE A (6 polls)  connected, payload NOT launched      <- the control
#     ---- remote_run() ----   ZERO POINT
#     PHASE B (12 polls) connected, payload BURNING           <- the measurement
#     ---- disconnect() ----
#     PHASE C (6 polls)  NOT connected, payload STILL BURNING <- the daemon
#                                                                question in
#                                                                miniature
#
#   Z->A isolates what an open connection alone costs (so a rise in B cannot
#   be blamed on the session). A->B is the experiment. B->C separates "the
#   remote WORK bills me" from "the open CONNECTION bills me".
#
#   THE PAYLOAD is five lines of pure arithmetic - no log(), no get_cpu(), no
#   API call of any kind, so it cannot perturb any meter through
#   instrumentation. It is a BOUNDED 3000-iteration burst (~9000 charged
#   lines): ~900s on a base-tier box, ~225s at Mk.III, ~56s at Mk.V. Bounded
#   on purpose - it self-terminates on every tier, but outlasts this script's
#   ~30s measurement window on every tier too. It is written with
#   write_file() (2 ticks, noise 2.0), NOT save()+upload() (10-50 ticks,
#   noise 2.0) - same result, an order of magnitude cheaper, and it leaves
#   nothing on the local disk.
#
# ===========================================================================
# HOW TO READ THE OUTPUT - decision table
#   Zbar = mean of the 4 PHASE Z readings (expect ~10)
#   Abar = mean of the 6 PHASE A readings (the control level)
#   B0,B1,B2 = the FIRST THREE PHASE B readings - these are the ones that
#   matter; a saturating burst moves a meter within a second or two.
#
#   FIRST, THE VALIDITY GATE - check this BEFORE reading any cpu number:
#     "T27 LAUNCH rr=true"  AND  "T27 MID ram_used=" GREATER than the
#     pre-launch ram_used in the "T27 SPECS" line  AND  "T27 LIVE
#     relaunch=false".
#     If rr=false the payload never started and PHASE B measures NOTHING -
#     the run is VOID, not a confirmation. If rr=true but ram_used never
#     moved and relaunch=true, the payload started and already FINISHED (or
#     was killed on the remote box) before the window - also VOID, re-run
#     against a slower box or report it, because "remote payloads are
#     themselves subject to the 5s overload kill" is a finding in its own
#     right and one this design cannot otherwise see.
#
#   CONFIRMS - REMOTE BILLING (docs correct):
#     all 12 PHASE B readings within 15 points of Abar, AND the final
#     "T27 DONE" line prints. Remote execution contributes nothing to the
#     local meter; planted daemons, remote payloads and background remote
#     jobs are retired as variance suspects for good, and the `daemons`
#     assertion in field_notes.txt:251 is upgraded from claim to measurement
#     by the "T27 DAEMONS mine=" line.
#
#   REFUTES - LOCAL BILLING:
#     any of B0/B1/B2 at or above Abar + 40, OR this script is KILLED during
#     PHASE B (last line printed is "T27 LAUNCH" or a "T27 B n=" line, and an
#     overload banner follows). Three official statements are wrong at once,
#     and anything running remotely becomes a first-class explanation for
#     load the player cannot see. STOP THE PROGRAMME AND REPORT - do not run
#     the repeat; this reopens daemons, background jobs and the whole scope
#     question across the entire investigation.
#
#   B HIGH, C BACK AT Abar:
#     the payload bills you only while the connection is open. The cost is
#     attached to the SESSION, not to the remote process. Different fix,
#     same severity - a long-lived connection becomes a load source.
#
#   B AT FLOOR, C HIGH:
#     nonsense under every model on the table; report verbatim, suspect the
#     disconnect() itself.
#
#   Zbar noticeably below Abar (say 10 vs 30) with B flat:
#     remote billing confirmed AND an open connection has a real standing
#     local cost - that is T26's question answered inside this run, and it
#     would be the first measured evidence that connect() leaves a
#     continuing load rather than a one-off 5-tick charge.
#
#   ANY reading that is neither ~10 nor ~Abar nor ~100 - report it VERBATIM
#   at full float precision. 93.75 is 100*15/16, 99.9984741210938 is
#   100*(1-2^-16); the trailing digits are the fingerprint of the underlying
#   arithmetic and rounding destroys them.
#
# ===========================================================================
# HOW TO RUN  (@param binds POSITIONALLY - there is no -flag= syntax)
#
#   PRECONDITIONS - check all four or the run is wasted:
#     1. Pivot Source is owned (remote_run() needs it). If not, the script
#        prints "T27 remote_run RAISED ..." and the run is void.
#     2. A target whose account you have ALREADY CRACKED. Pass it as
#        user@ip. The script aborts loudly if conn.user comes back empty,
#        because connect().success only means the port was reachable
#        (web_api.txt:174).
#     3. That target's trace ceiling is at least 90s - the script stays
#        connected ~60s and adds ~7.5 noise (connect 0.5 + write_file 2.0 +
#        remote_run 2.5 + remote_run 2.5). It reads conn.trace_time and
#        aborts itself if the ceiling is too low, before doing anything noisy.
#     4. You do not mind a 5-line file being left in that user's home
#        directory, and a bounded arithmetic loop burning on that box for up
#        to ~15 minutes after you leave.
#
#   BEFORE THE FIRST RUN, paste these once: `neofetch` (or `sysinfo`) for
#   your own CPU tier, then `ps` and `jobs` to confirm nothing else of yours
#   is running.
#
#     run T27 user@1.2.3.4
#
#   The default remote path is <remote home>/t27_burn.bd, built from
#   get_cwd(conn). If "T27 write_file" reports success=false, re-run with an
#   explicit writable path as the SECOND positional argument:
#
#     run T27 user@1.2.3.4 /home/anon42/t27_burn.bd
#
#   AFTERWARDS: reconnect and `rm` the file when you want it gone. Do not
#   delete it during the run - the payload is already loaded, but deleting it
#   would break the PHASE L relaunch probe that proves the payload is alive.
#
#   REPEATS: 2 runs, ~80s each. The outcome is close to binary (floor versus
#   saturation) and the predicted effect is enormous next to this regime's
#   known run-to-run spread, so one clean confirming run plus one repeat
#   settles it. If run 1 shows LOCAL BILLING, STOP AT ONE and report
#   immediately - that result changes what the rest of the programme should
#   be testing. If run 1 trips the validity gate, the repeat is mandatory.
#
# ===========================================================================
# PACING DISCIPLINE (every line costs a tick; unpaced bursts kill)
#   There is no unpaced burst anywhere in this file - nothing in it is
#   supposed to die. The longest run of executed lines between sleeps is
#   FOUR. The poll loops are a 3-line body behind sleep(1), the same shape
#   that held a steady 10.0 for 16+ seconds in test_cpu_decay.bd. The only
#   deliberately unpaced pair is remote_run() and the "T27 LAUNCH" line
#   immediately after it, so that the zero point is timestamped to the tick
#   rather than a second later.
#   ALL abort paths are paced and print a labelled "T27 ABORT" line before
#   exit(), so a failed precondition is instantly identifiable and never
#   masquerades as a null result.
#
# OBSERVER EFFECT
#   The poll loop is IDENTICAL in all four phases, so whatever log() and
#   get_cpu() cost, they cost it equally on both sides of every boundary; the
#   claim is a step change within one run, not an absolute level. The payload
#   contains no log() and no get_cpu() at all. The expensive calls -
#   write_file (2 ticks), server_specs (1 tick), remote_run (2 ticks) - are
#   separated from every poll window by sleeps, and PHASE A is sampled AFTER
#   write_file has settled precisely so the control level is already back at
#   its floor when the launch happens. get_trace_time() and ram_used are read
#   only in the between-phase checkpoint lines, never inside a poll loop, so
#   the measured loop body is never widened.
#
# IF IT IS KILLED MID-RUN
#   Every line carries a cumulative t=+Ns and its phase letter, so the last
#   printed line names exactly where and when it died. A kill during PHASE B
#   is the REFUTING result, not a failure - paste the overload banner too.
#   Then, because "[SYSTEM] CPU overloaded. All processes terminated." has
#   never had its scope measured: run `ps`, reconnect to the target, and
#   check server_specs().ram_used. If the remote payload died with your local
#   script, the kill crosses machine boundaries, which is a second headline
#   result hiding inside the first.
#
# @param host "cracked target as user@ip - e.g. anon42@45.33.12.7"
# @param rpath "writable remote path for the payload" optional
# ===========================================================================

var t0 = get_unix_time()
var c0 = get_cpu()
log("T27 start cpu0=" + str(c0) + " (line-2 reading, before anything settles)")
sleep(1)
var h = get_param("host", "").replace("\"", "").trim()
var rp = get_param("rpath", "").replace("\"", "").trim()
sleep(1)
var n = 0
var a = 0
var b = 0
sleep(1)
var c = 6
var d = 0
var conn = none
sleep(1)
var ok = false
var wrote = false
var rr = false
sleep(1)
var r2 = false
var sp = none
var ru0 = -1
sleep(1)
var wf = none
var dl = []
sleep(1)
var p = "var i = 0\nvar x = 0\nwhile i < 3000:\n    x = i * 2\n    i = i + 1\n"
sleep(1)

if h == "":
    log("T27 ABORT no host - run: run T27 user@1.2.3.4")
    exit()
sleep(1)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The daemon assertion, finally MEASURED. daemons() is 0 ticks and needs no
# connection. field_notes.txt:251 retires planted daemons on a player claim;
# this line is the first time that claim has ever been checked in-game.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
try:
    dl = daemons()
catch e:
    log("T27 DAEMONS unavailable (Daemon Source not owned?) " + str(e.message))
sleep(1)
log("T27 DAEMONS mine=" + str(len(dl)) + "  0 = the 'no planted daemons' assertion is now measured, not asserted")
sleep(1)
sleep(1)

# ===========================================================================
# PHASE Z - local floor, NOT connected. The anchor. Expect ~10.
# ===========================================================================
while n < 4:
    sleep(1)
    log("T27 Z n=" + str(n) + " t=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + "s cpu=" + str(get_cpu()))
    n = n + 1

sleep(1)
log("T27 Z done - pre-connect floor established")
sleep(1)

# ===========================================================================
# CONNECT. .success only means the port answered - .user != "" is the only
# real proof of login (web_api.txt:174).
# ===========================================================================
try:
    conn = connect(h, 22)
    ok = conn.success
catch e:
    log("T27 connect RAISED " + str(e.message))
sleep(2)

if not ok:
    log("T27 ABORT connect failed t=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + "s")
    exit()
sleep(1)

log("T27 CONN user=" + str(conn.user) + " host=" + str(conn.hostname) + " trace_ceiling=" + str(conn.trace_time) + " t=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + "s")
sleep(1)

if conn.user == "":
    log("T27 ABORT connected but NOT authenticated - crack this account first")
    disconnect(conn)
    exit()
sleep(1)

if conn.trace_time < 90:
    log("T27 ABORT trace ceiling too low for a 60s visit - pick another target")
    disconnect(conn)
    exit()
sleep(1)

if rp == "":
    rp = get_cwd(conn) + "/t27_burn.bd"
sleep(1)

sp = server_specs(conn)
sleep(1)
ru0 = sp.ram_used
sleep(1)
log("T27 SPECS cpu_tier=" + str(sp.cpu_tier) + " cpu_ticks=" + str(sp.cpu_ticks) + " ram_slots=" + str(sp.ram_slots) + " ram_used=" + str(ru0) + " max_daemons=" + str(sp.max_daemons) + " daemons_used=" + str(sp.daemons_used))
sleep(1)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Plant the payload. write_file() is 2 ticks / noise 2.0 - a tenth the tick
# cost of save()+upload() for an identical outcome, and no local file.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
try:
    wf = write_file(conn, rp, p)
    wrote = wf.success
catch e:
    log("T27 write_file RAISED " + str(e.message))
sleep(2)

log("T27 WROTE success=" + str(wrote) + " path=" + str(rp) + " t=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + "s")
sleep(1)

if not wrote:
    log("T27 ABORT payload not written - re-run with an explicit 2nd arg path")
    disconnect(conn)
    exit()
sleep(1)
sleep(1)

# ===========================================================================
# PHASE A - connected, payload NOT launched. THE CONTROL LEVEL.
# Sampled after write_file() has settled, so the pre-launch level is already
# back at its floor when the zero point arrives.
# ===========================================================================
while a < 6:
    sleep(1)
    log("T27 A n=" + str(a) + " t=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + "s cpu=" + str(get_cpu()))
    a = a + 1

sleep(1)
log("T27 A done trace=" + str(get_trace_time()) + " - control level established, launching next")
sleep(1)

# ===========================================================================
# LAUNCH - the zero point. remote_run() and its log line are the one
# deliberately unpaced pair in this file, so the timestamp is exact.
# rr is CAPTURED, not discarded: rr=false means PHASE B measures nothing.
# ===========================================================================
try:
    rr = remote_run(conn, rp)
catch e:
    log("T27 remote_run RAISED " + str(e.message) + " (Pivot Source not owned?)")
log("T27 LAUNCH rr=" + str(rr) + " t=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + "s <- ZERO POINT")

if not rr:
    log("T27 WARNING rr=false - payload did NOT start. PHASE B is now a NULL CONTROL, not a confirmation. Check ram_slots/ram_used above and Pivot Source.")
sleep(1)

# ===========================================================================
# PHASE B - connected, payload BURNING. THE MEASUREMENT.
# B n=0,1,2 are the ones that decide it. Split in two halves so a remote
# liveness reading lands mid-window without ever widening the poll body.
# ===========================================================================
while b < 6:
    sleep(1)
    log("T27 B n=" + str(b) + " t=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + "s cpu=" + str(get_cpu()))
    b = b + 1

sleep(1)
sp = server_specs(conn)
sleep(1)
log("T27 MID ram_used=" + str(sp.ram_used) + " was=" + str(ru0) + " trace=" + str(get_trace_time()) + " t=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + "s")
sleep(1)
sleep(1)

while c < 12:
    sleep(1)
    log("T27 B n=" + str(c) + " t=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + "s cpu=" + str(get_cpu()))
    c = c + 1

sleep(1)
log("T27 B done - 12 polls, not killed")
sleep(1)

# ===========================================================================
# PHASE L - liveness, the second independent proof the payload is alive.
# web_api.txt:228: remote_run() returns false if the script "is already
# running". So relaunch=false + ram_used still elevated = STILL BURNING.
# relaunch=true = the first payload had already ended, and a second one has
# now started (which makes PHASE C a freshly-launched burn instead - still
# interpretable, just say so).
# ===========================================================================
try:
    r2 = remote_run(conn, rp)
catch e:
    log("T27 relaunch RAISED " + str(e.message))
sleep(1)
sp = server_specs(conn)
sleep(1)
log("T27 LIVE relaunch=" + str(r2) + " ram_used=" + str(sp.ram_used) + " was=" + str(ru0) + " (false + elevated = payload STILL RUNNING)")
sleep(1)
sleep(1)

# ===========================================================================
# DISCONNECT - payload keeps burning, session is gone.
# ===========================================================================
disconnect(conn)
log("T27 DISCONNECTED t=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + "s - payload still burning remotely")
sleep(1)
sleep(1)

# ===========================================================================
# PHASE C - NOT connected, payload STILL BURNING. Separates "the remote work
# bills me" from "the open connection bills me". This is the daemon question
# in miniature: a process running on someone else's box while you are gone.
# ===========================================================================
while d < 6:
    sleep(1)
    log("T27 C n=" + str(d) + " t=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + "s cpu=" + str(get_cpu()))
    d = d + 1

sleep(1)
log("T27 DONE t=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + "s - local script SURVIVED the whole remote burn. Paste every T27 line, in order, at FULL float precision. Do not round.")
